think The recursion prompt points at the Rye-first crypto parity stream — the last ten commits are Rye-native crypto compositions, parity-GREEN. The SEC1 pubkey commit named BIP-32 hierarchical-deterministic keys outright as the coming rung. Lindy-first crux-first: BIP-32 is the highest-Lindy next thread — a wallet's whole account model stands on it, and it composes only stones already GREEN.
think Studied the composition idiom from secp256k1_pubkey.rye and its witness: pure-Rye module with an opening triad, contract asserts, a self-contained selftest printing GREEN crypto-<name>, and a per-file *_witness.rish that builds fresh and greps the GREEN line.
think Confirmed every stone: HMAC-SHA-512 mac(msg,key)→[64]u8, secp256k1_scalar reduce (64-byte LITTLE-endian in, LE out) for (I_L + k_par) mod n since no public add exists, from_private_compressed for serP(point(k)), bitcoin_address hash160 for the fingerprint, encoding_base58check.encode(version_byte, payload) for the xprv/xpub envelope. is_canonical(&LE) gates I_L < n.
think Scoped the lap deliberately: master_from_seed + ckd_priv (hardened and normal) + neuter + xprv/xpub Base58Check serialization. This reaches EVERY published Test Vector 1 value. CKDpub (public-parent→public-child point addition) is a distinct capability whose outputs are all covered by neutering here, so I named it the next rung rather than widening this round (narrow-scope, finishing edge).
think Did NOT recite the BIP-32 test-vector base58 strings from memory — a single wrong character makes the parity dishonest. Fetched Test Vector 1 byte-exact from the canonical bitcoin/bips bip-0032.mediawiki source.
obs The scalar add rides the GREEN reduce: (I_L + k_par) is at most 2n < 2^257, laid into the low bytes of a 64-byte little-endian wide buffer with a single carry byte, reduced, then mirrored back to big-endian. Authors no new primitive.
obs GREEN on the first metal build — all six chain nodes (m, m/0H, m/0H/1, m/0H/1/2H, m/0H/1/2H/2, m/0H/1/2H/2/1000000000) serialize to EXACTLY the spec's published xprv AND xpub strings, character for character. A one-bit error in the HMAC, the modular add, the compression, the fingerprint, or the envelope would break a match. Edge refusals BadSeedLength fire for a too-short and a too-long seed.
